## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly multi-tenant flow with prompt catalog versioning, failover, and privacy controls in Azure OpenAI.

## Key Concepts

- API Management routing with tenant context
- Versioned prompt catalog stored in Azure Blob
- Per-tenant quotas and graceful fallback
- PII redaction via Text Analytics before OpenAI calls
- Regional OpenAI endpoints and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
async function routeRequest(tenantId, payload) {
  const tmpl = await blob.get(`prompts/${tenantId}/v1.json`);
  const redacted = pii.redact(payload.prompt, payload.user);
  const finalPrompt = `${tmpl.promptPrefix}\n${redacted}`;
  return openai.chat(finalPrompt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test failover if the catalog fetch fails?
- How would you monitor per-tenant quotas and trigger alerts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compliant, production-grade retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) assistant for contract processing. It evaluates understanding of multi-tenant security, data isolation, and regulatory compliance patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) with Cognitive Search knowledge base
- Per-tenant isolation via Key Vault and customer-managed keys (CMK)
- PII redaction with consent-based access control
- Policy engine to detect and block sensitive prompt patterns
- End-to-end audit trail to Data Lake for compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode policy snippet
function isAllowed(prompt, piiPresent, consentGiven) {
  if (piiPresent && !consentGiven) {
    return { allowed: false, reason: 'PII detected without consent' };
  }
  if (containsSensitivePatterns(prompt)) {
    return { allowed: false, reason: 'Sensitive content blocked' };
  }
  return { allowed: true };
}
```
